Moth uses constructor syntax for struct and choice arguments and for the named entries of an anonymous record. Pipes enclose a declaration's fields. Parentheses supply constructor arguments or group an expression.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Size = |
        width I32,
        height I32,
    |

    size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720)

    settings #= (
        title = "Strategy",
        window = (width = 1280, height = 720),
    )
]

Every anonymous field has a name and a value. Records can nest directly.
`#=` requires all their values to be known during compilation.

`(value)` groups an expression. `(name = value)` constructs a one-field record.
Commas separate entries, and a final comma is optional. Moth has no tuple values.
The label and `=` share one logical line. The value may start on the next line.

Named types still need their constructors. An anonymous record with matching
fields does not become a `Size`.

Moth Object Notation (MON) uses the same named entries for literal data.
Suppose a program reads a MON file using a schema for `Size`. The file can
contain this record:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    (width = 1280, height = 720)
]

The schema identifies the two fields as a `Size`. In Moth source, write
`Size(...)` to choose that named type. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format)
for the data rules.
